WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.720
- This windswept beach in
the south-east of Scotland

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.090
is open to the public.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.255
But to get here, there's
a bit of effort required.

00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:08.088
[waves crash]

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.210
That is part of the Elie Chainwalk,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.530
500 metres long, made up of eight chains

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.580
anchored into the cliff face,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.660
along with some carved foot and handholds.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.440
It was set up in the 1920s,
likely to help local fishermen

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.910
get down the cliffs to their boats.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.740
A century later it's maintained
as a local attraction

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.010
and as optional side trip
on the local coast path,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.908
which means that in any weather,

00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:36.170
even on very blustery, ill-advised,
freezing days like this,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.830
you can just scramble along the Chain Walk

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.580
as if it was any other public footpath.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.490
Now, you should not do this alone.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.620
I've got a local guide with me.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:43.650
Thank you, Allan.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.860
And before we set off, I asked
him about how all this works.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.230
- It's open to the public,
completely open access all year round.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.340
You do need a certain
level of physical ability.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.880
You're putting a lot of
your weight onto your arms.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.170
There's a couple of chains

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.170
you have to climb and descend
from a reasonably high height.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.290
So although it's
technically not difficult,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.800
you do have to be cautious
climbing at height.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.820
But if you come here in the
summer, you'll see families

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:10.960
tackling the chains,

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.200
but also groups come here with helmets
and harnesses, and so on.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.440
A wide range of people come
and give the chains a go.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.970
- Very occasionally, people do fall here.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.750
Perhaps because they've
ignored safety signs,

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.620
or strayed off the path,
or been trapped by the tide

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.380
and had to call out the local lifeboat.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.570
The Chain Walk isn't massively dangerous,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.050
it's not just for climbing experts,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.980
but it is dangerous enough
that the safety signs are justified.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.900
There are modern attractions
like this all over the world

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.100
but they generally use harness systems,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.310
instead of "hold on tight
and watch out for the tide".

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.300
The Chain Walk sits in that strange group

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.028
of lesser-known places where
it would never be created now.

00:01:48.028 --> 00:01:51.160
But it does exist, and
no-one wants to get rid of it.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.570
It was repaired about ten years ago

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.910
after someone stole the chains,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.709
and every six months
engineers come out to check

00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:00.180
that everything is still
anchored in, safe and intact.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.849
- One thing you might notice:
that some of the fixing points move.

00:02:03.849 --> 00:02:06.610
So quite often, we'll have
people reporting them in,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.920
but that's actually meant to be like that.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.710
There's meant to be a
little bit of movement

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.120
so it doesn't stress the rock as much.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.560
I've been coming here and leading
groups here for about 30 years.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.510
Plan ahead.
And secondly, come prepared.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.830
Decent footwear, know the tides,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.200
know what the weather's going to be like

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.000
so that you don't get surprised
as you go along the chains.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.630
There have been reports of people falling.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.558
They see the tide, they feel trapped.

00:02:32.558 --> 00:02:33.860
They don't want to get wet,

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.760
so they decide to climb up the cliffs.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.440
But if you know the tides,
and if you plan around that

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.210
then that's not going to happen.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.700
When I first came here,
it was the original chains

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.510
which were old and rusty.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.710
The ones there now are quite
new and shiny and solid.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.020
- And what I love most about this

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.980
is the Chain Walk doesn't
really lead anywhere important.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.884
You go across some cliffs,
down a bit, across, up again,

00:02:56.884 --> 00:02:58.880
and you get access to a couple of beaches

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.870
that are pretty much like
every other beach around here.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.798
People do this, people take a
small but significant risk

00:03:04.798 --> 00:03:08.602
and take the Chain Walk
for no other reason than...

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.063
it's there.

